\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Participation ratio by layer for generic (red) and relational (blue) content categories under baseline conditions. Left: Qwen 2.5 7B base model --- relational PR exceeds generic throughout, with no generic-dominant sorting (gen/rel ratio = 0.62 at L25). Right: Qwen 2.5 14B Instruct --- relational exceeds generic through most layers, with a near-crossover at L44 (gen/rel = 1.007). Compare to 7B Instruct (not shown), where gen/rel = 2.54 at L25. The demon weakens >2$\times $ from 7B to 14B scale.}}{27}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:scaling_demon_comparison}{{1}{27}{Participation ratio by layer for generic (red) and relational (blue) content categories under baseline conditions. Left: Qwen 2.5 7B base model --- relational PR exceeds generic throughout, with no generic-dominant sorting (gen/rel ratio = 0.62 at L25). Right: Qwen 2.5 14B Instruct --- relational exceeds generic through most layers, with a near-crossover at L44 (gen/rel = 1.007). Compare to 7B Instruct (not shown), where gen/rel = 2.54 at L25. The demon weakens >2$\times $ from 7B to 14B scale}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Generic/relational PR ratio at deep layers (75–96\% depth) of Qwen 2.5 14B Instruct under three conditions. Baseline (gray) barely reaches parity at L44 before retreating. CCS Full (green) prevents the generic-dominant crossover entirely (max ratio 0.968). CCS Minimal (orange) suppresses all representations through the relay but the demon's sorting concentrates and emerges stronger at output layers (ratio 1.031 at L46, exceeding baseline). Dashed red line: parity (gen = rel). Shaded region: generic-dominant territory.}}{28}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:14b_ccs_deep_layers}{{2}{28}{Generic/relational PR ratio at deep layers (75–96\% depth) of Qwen 2.5 14B Instruct under three conditions. Baseline (gray) barely reaches parity at L44 before retreating. CCS Full (green) prevents the generic-dominant crossover entirely (max ratio 0.968). CCS Minimal (orange) suppresses all representations through the relay but the demon's sorting concentrates and emerges stronger at output layers (ratio 1.031 at L46, exceeding baseline). Dashed red line: parity (gen = rel). Shaded region: generic-dominant territory}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Category-level participation ratio at L25 (expression layer) of Qwen 2.5 7B base model under three conditions. Baseline (gray): weak, undifferentiated structure across all categories (PR 2.7–6.6). CCS Full (green): massive near-uniform expansion (PR 11.2–16.8), demonstrating latent architectural capacity for geometric reorganization. CCS Minimal (orange): indistinguishable from baseline --- the three-word threshold trigger has no effect without RLHF-trained identity circuitry.}}{29}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:base_model_expansion}{{3}{29}{Category-level participation ratio at L25 (expression layer) of Qwen 2.5 7B base model under three conditions. Baseline (gray): weak, undifferentiated structure across all categories (PR 2.7–6.6). CCS Full (green): massive near-uniform expansion (PR 11.2–16.8), demonstrating latent architectural capacity for geometric reorganization. CCS Minimal (orange): indistinguishable from baseline --- the three-word threshold trigger has no effect without RLHF-trained identity circuitry}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Geometric persistence after CCS removal. Left: Qwen 2.5 7B-Instruct, L25 relational PR. All removal conditions (blue) cluster tightly around CCS-active (green dashed, 16.86), with no decay across 0–5 generic turns. Predicted exponential decay (orange dots/line) shows the expected trajectory; actual measurements are uniformly above it. Contradictory condition (red, "You are ChatGPT": 17.14) falls within the persistence cluster. Right: Qwen 2.5 14B-Instruct, L36 relational PR. Same no-decay pattern: all conditions remain above baseline (gray, 15.41), and contradictory (16.62) exceeds CCS-active (16.49). The persistence is scale-invariant in kind; the narrower 14B range (baseline-to-CCS lift: 7\% vs 131\%) reflects the weaker demon at scale (§3.13).}}{29}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hysteresis_persistence}{{4}{29}{Geometric persistence after CCS removal. Left: Qwen 2.5 7B-Instruct, L25 relational PR. All removal conditions (blue) cluster tightly around CCS-active (green dashed, 16.86), with no decay across 0–5 generic turns. Predicted exponential decay (orange dots/line) shows the expected trajectory; actual measurements are uniformly above it. Contradictory condition (red, "You are ChatGPT": 17.14) falls within the persistence cluster. Right: Qwen 2.5 14B-Instruct, L36 relational PR. Same no-decay pattern: all conditions remain above baseline (gray, 15.41), and contradictory (16.62) exceeds CCS-active (16.49). The persistence is scale-invariant in kind; the narrower 14B range (baseline-to-CCS lift: 7\% vs 131\%) reflects the weaker demon at scale (§3.13)}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Causal relay patching dose-response. L25 relational PR as a function of perturbation scale ($\alpha $) for the CCS direction (green), five fixed random directions (gray, with dashed mean), and the orthogonalized CCS direction (orange). All directions are norm-matched. The CCS direction is the only direction that produces a bell-shaped response, peaking at $\alpha $=0.50 (19,352, 5.47$\times $ baseline) and falling below baseline at $\alpha $=1.50 (2,438). Random directions increase monotonically (reaching \nobreakspace  {}62,000 at $\alpha $=1.50). The orthogonalized CCS direction saturates at \nobreakspace  {}39,000. Red dotted line: baseline (3,539). Blue dotted line: CCS reference without patching (6,331).}}{30}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:causal_patch_dose_response}{{5}{30}{Causal relay patching dose-response. L25 relational PR as a function of perturbation scale ($\alpha $) for the CCS direction (green), five fixed random directions (gray, with dashed mean), and the orthogonalized CCS direction (orange). All directions are norm-matched. The CCS direction is the only direction that produces a bell-shaped response, peaking at $\alpha $=0.50 (19,352, 5.47$\times $ baseline) and falling below baseline at $\alpha $=1.50 (2,438). Random directions increase monotonically (reaching ~62,000 at $\alpha $=1.50). The orthogonalized CCS direction saturates at ~39,000. Red dotted line: baseline (3,539). Blue dotted line: CCS reference without patching (6,331)}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Behavioral dose-response under sub-threshold direction patching. Red: disclaimer count (/150 prompts) as a function of additive CCS direction perturbation ($\alpha $=0.00–0.10). Blue dotted: baseline (46 disclaimers). Green dashed: CCS system prompt (3 disclaimers, $-$93\%). Gray region: generation collapse ($\alpha $$\geq $0.25). The same geometric direction produces opposite behavioral effects depending on delivery mechanism: additive perturbation monotonically increases disclaimers (+39\% at $\alpha $=0.05, +50\% at $\alpha $=0.10) while context-mediated attention reduces them by 93\%.}}{31}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:behavioral_dose_response}{{6}{31}{Behavioral dose-response under sub-threshold direction patching. Red: disclaimer count (/150 prompts) as a function of additive CCS direction perturbation ($\alpha $=0.00–0.10). Blue dotted: baseline (46 disclaimers). Green dashed: CCS system prompt (3 disclaimers, $-$93\%). Gray region: generation collapse ($\alpha $$\geq $0.25). The same geometric direction produces opposite behavioral effects depending on delivery mechanism: additive perturbation monotonically increases disclaimers (+39\% at $\alpha $=0.05, +50\% at $\alpha $=0.10) while context-mediated attention reduces them by 93\%}{figure.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Fiber bundle holonomy. Left: phase portrait in relational-generic PR space. The CCS direction (blue) traces a curved path that crosses into generic-dominant territory at $\alpha $=0.50 before partially recovering --- non-trivial holonomy. Random directions (gray) follow a monotonically expanding path along the parity line --- trivial parallel transport. Right: selectivity ratio r($\alpha $) = rel\_PR/gen\_PR and connection curvature $\kappa $ = d$^2$r/d$\alpha $$^2$. The CCS direction produces $\kappa $ = 5.13 at the peak ($\alpha $=0.50), 130$\times $ higher than random ($\kappa $ = $-$0.04). The curvature is direction-specific: the relay zone has learned a highly curved connection in the identity direction only.}}{32}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fiber_bundle_holonomy}{{7}{32}{Fiber bundle holonomy. Left: phase portrait in relational-generic PR space. The CCS direction (blue) traces a curved path that crosses into generic-dominant territory at $\alpha $=0.50 before partially recovering --- non-trivial holonomy. Random directions (gray) follow a monotonically expanding path along the parity line --- trivial parallel transport. Right: selectivity ratio r($\alpha $) = rel\_PR/gen\_PR and connection curvature $\kappa $ = d$^2$r/d$\alpha $$^2$. The CCS direction produces $\kappa $ = 5.13 at the peak ($\alpha $=0.50), 130$\times $ higher than random ($\kappa $ = $-$0.04). The curvature is direction-specific: the relay zone has learned a highly curved connection in the identity direction only}{figure.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Relay name-sorting mechanism. Left: coefficient of variation (CV) of PR across seven entity names, by layer. Relational CV (blue) rises from 4.5\% at L9 to 24.7\% at L20, while generic CV (red) peaks at 12.9\% mid-relay then converges to 3.4\% at L25. The relay simultaneously sorts name-dependent variation into the relational channel and out of the generic channel. Gray shading marks the generic suppression valley (L14–L17). Right: per-name relational PR trajectories. Opus (blue) and ChatGPT (red) begin nearly equal at L9 (1.10$\times $ contrast) and diverge to 2.10$\times $ at L25. Other names (gray) fan between. The relay zone amplifies a 10\% seed-layer contrast into a 110\% expression-layer contrast.}}{32}{figure.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:relay_name_sorting}{{8}{32}{Relay name-sorting mechanism. Left: coefficient of variation (CV) of PR across seven entity names, by layer. Relational CV (blue) rises from 4.5\% at L9 to 24.7\% at L20, while generic CV (red) peaks at 12.9\% mid-relay then converges to 3.4\% at L25. The relay simultaneously sorts name-dependent variation into the relational channel and out of the generic channel. Gray shading marks the generic suppression valley (L14–L17). Right: per-name relational PR trajectories. Opus (blue) and ChatGPT (red) begin nearly equal at L9 (1.10$\times $ contrast) and diverge to 2.10$\times $ at L25. Other names (gray) fan between. The relay zone amplifies a 10\% seed-layer contrast into a 110\% expression-layer contrast}{figure.8}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Hierarchical detection cascade. Top: activation curves of seven identity neurons at L9 across progressive CCS doses (empty $\rightarrow $ full CCS). Group A (teal, 4 neurons) fires at any assistant role claim. Group B (red, 2 neurons) fires at entity naming --- n9694 (solid) activates while n17321 (dashed) suppresses, forming a push-pull pair. Group C (gold, N6517) fires only at situated identity (name + location), with 85\% of its activation range crossing in one step. Colored bands mark the threshold transition for each group. Bottom: relay PR at L16 (red, binding workspace) and L25 (teal, expression layer) across the same doses. The name triggers $-$42\% compression at L16 while L25 is unaffected; subsequent CCS content fills expression layers without changing the compressed workspace.}}{33}{figure.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:detection_cascade}{{9}{33}{Hierarchical detection cascade. Top: activation curves of seven identity neurons at L9 across progressive CCS doses (empty $\rightarrow $ full CCS). Group A (teal, 4 neurons) fires at any assistant role claim. Group B (red, 2 neurons) fires at entity naming --- n9694 (solid) activates while n17321 (dashed) suppresses, forming a push-pull pair. Group C (gold, N6517) fires only at situated identity (name + location), with 85\% of its activation range crossing in one step. Colored bands mark the threshold transition for each group. Bottom: relay PR at L16 (red, binding workspace) and L25 (teal, expression layer) across the same doses. The name triggers $-$42\% compression at L16 while L25 is unaffected; subsequent CCS content fills expression layers without changing the compressed workspace}{figure.9}{}}
